<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="A 1993 Colnago in Tange Prestige steel with Shimano Dura-Ace 7400-series components and Campagnolo Shamal deep-section wheels."/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:title" content="1993 Colnago · Tange Prestige and Dura-Ace"/><meta property="og:description" content="Italian framebuilding, Japanese steel and two early-1990s systems meet in one Colnago."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/bikes/colnago-tange-prestige/hero.webp"/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><title>1993 Colnago · Tange Prestige and Dura-Ace</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head>
<body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/colnago-1993-tange-prestige/"/><main class="atala-page">
  <header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">Cambiago, Italy · 1993</p><h1>Colnago<br/><em>Tange Prestige</em></h1><p>An Italian racing bicycle built in Japanese steel and fitted with two defining technical strands from the early 1990s: Shimano’s Dura-Ace 7400 series and Campagnolo’s deep-section Shamal wheels.</p></header>
  <figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${images}/hero.webp`} alt="Red and cream 1993 Colnago viewed from the drive side"/><figcaption>The 1993 Colnago with chrome fork, Tange Prestige frame and Campagnolo Shamal wheels. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="atala-facts" aria-label="Key facts"><div><strong>1993</strong><span>Stated year</span></div><div><strong>Tange Prestige</strong><span>Japanese steel tubing</span></div><div><strong>Dura-Ace</strong><span>7400 series</span></div><div><strong>54 cm</strong><span>Frame size</span></div></section>
  <section class="atala-opening"><p class="section-label">Two traditions</p><p>The Colnago name is Italian, but the tubing decal points to Japan. Tange Prestige was a light, heat-treated tubeset, giving this bicycle a different combination from the Columbus frames most readily associated with Cambiago.</p></section>
  <section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">Colnago</p><h2>From workshop to international marque</h2></div><div><p>Ernesto Colnago founded the company in Cambiago near Milan. Through professional teams and a close eye for paint, logos and mechanical detail, Colnago became one of the best-known names in Italian cycling.</p><p>This bicycle sits between the classic steel racer and the systems thinking of the 1990s. Its frame remains lugged steel, while its gears and wheels show components increasingly developed as complete, coordinated solutions.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-details"><figure><img src={`${images}/frame.webp`} alt="Red and cream paintwork on the Colnago frame" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Colnago clovers and lettering in a red-and-cream finish with chrome rear stays.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/cockpit.webp`} alt="Cinelli bar and stem with integrated Dura-Ace levers" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Cinelli Campione del Mondo bars and a Cinelli stem above the traditional threaded headset.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/drivetrain.webp`} alt="Shimano Dura-Ace chainset and rear derailleur" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The Dura-Ace 7400 family brings together the drivetrain, gears and brakes.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">Shimano Dura-Ace</p><h2>From indexed down-tube shifting to STI</h2></div><div><p>Dura-Ace 7400 became Shimano’s decisive professional road group. It began with indexed down-tube shifting and later gained STI, moving the gear control into the brake lever.</p><p>The supplied specification refers to both 7401 and 7403, as well as the final generation of indexed down-tube shifters. The levers visible here are integrated. The bicycle is therefore described as a 7400-series build without assigning certainty to every individual model number.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-detail-pair"><figure><img src={`${images}/shamal.webp`} alt="Campagnolo Shamal deep-section rim and Dura-Ace brake" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The deep Shamal profile at the rear wheel beneath a Dura-Ace brake.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="section-label">Campagnolo Shamal</p><h2>The wheel became a complete product</h2><p>Campagnolo introduced the Shamal in 1992 as its first complete high-profile wheel. Rim, hub, spoke count and aerodynamic shape were conceived as one system rather than parts selected independently.</p><p>The supplied specification identifies the hubs on this bicycle as Campagnolo Record C. Its exact wheel construction should therefore be read as the present build, while the Shamal name and deep-section rims are clearly visible in the photographs.</p></div></section>
  <section class="koga-specs"><div><p class="section-label">The bicycle</p><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Marque and year</dt><dd>Colnago, 1993</dd></div><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Tange Prestige steel, size 54</dd></div><div><dt>Groupset</dt><dd>Shimano Dura-Ace 7400 series</dd></div><div><dt>Chainset</dt><dd>Dura-Ace, stated as 7401</dd></div><div><dt>Wheels</dt><dd>Campagnolo Shamal deep-section; hubs stated as Record C</dd></div><div><dt>Handlebar</dt><dd>Cinelli Campione del Mondo</dd></div><div><dt>Stem</dt><dd>Cinelli</dd></div></dl></section>
  <section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">Sources</p><h2>The basis of this page</h2></div><div><p>The year and specification come from the information supplied with the bicycle, compared with the photographs shown here. The operation of the Dura-Ace family was checked against Shimano service instructions for the 7400 series.</p><p>Campagnolo states that the Shamal was introduced in 1992 as the company’s first complete high-profile wheel. Where the supplied component list is internally unclear, the distinction is noted in the text.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-closing"><p>The bicycle joins three traditions: Colnago’s Italian frame design, Tange’s Japanese tubing and components from the years when gears and wheels were becoming complete systems.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1990-1999/`}>View the period 1990–1999 →</a></div></section>
</main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
